Since you already have BigHeading and SubText defined in your LoginStyles.js, you don't need new functions. You just need to update the values inside those existing styled components.
To get that professional look with the 20px/500 weight you asked for, update these two specific blocks in your LoginStyles.js file:
1. Update these in LoginStyles.js
Find these two functions in your file and replace them with this code:
export const BigHeading = styled(Typography)(({ theme }) => ({
  fontWeight: 800,           // Makes the "Welcome" part feel "big" and bold
  fontSize: "2.5rem",       // Increased size for professional impact
  lineHeight: 1.2,
  color: theme.palette.text.primary,
  letterSpacing: "-0.02em", // Tighter lettering looks more high-end
  [theme.breakpoints.down("sm")]: { fontSize: 24 },
}));

export const SubText = styled(Typography)(({ theme }) => ({
  fontSize: 20,             // As per your request
  fontWeight: 500,          // As per your request
  color: theme.palette.text.primary, 
  lineHeight: 1.5,          // Better spacing for long text
  opacity: 0.85,            // Professional look (not pure black/white)
  marginTop: theme.spacing(1),
}));

2. Update your LoginPort.js (The JSX)
Since we are not splitting the text anymore, your JSX remains very simple and clean. Make sure the LeftCol looks exactly like this to avoid the blank screen:
{/* LEFT COLUMN */}
<LeftCol>
  {/* The title stays big */}
  <BigHeading variant="h1">
    {content?.hero?.welcomeTitle}
  </BigHeading>
  
  {/* This long text will now be 20px and 500 weight */}
  <SubText>
    {content?.hero?.subText}
  </SubText>

  {/* Keep your Legal Warning section as is */}
  <Box sx={{ mt: 4 }}>
    <BigHeading sx={{ fontSize: '1.5rem' }}>
      {content?.legal?.title}
    </BigHeading>
    <FeatureList>
      {content?.legal?.items.map((item, idx) => (
        <FeatureItem key={idx}>
          <Bullet /> {item}
        </FeatureItem>
      ))}
    </FeatureList>
  </Box>
</LeftCol>

Why this fixes the "Blank Screen":
The blank screen usually happens when you try to use a component that isn't imported or defined. By using the existing BigHeading and SubText from your LoginStyles.js, you ensure the project recognizes the components.
Why this looks professional:
 * Contrast: BigHeading at 2.5rem creates a clear starting point for the user's eye.
 * Readability: SubText at 20px with 1.5 line height ensures that even if the text is long, it doesn't feel like a "wall of words."
 * Theme Integration: It uses theme.palette.text.primary, so if you ever change your app theme, this page will update automatically.
